WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 276: Neil Armstrong: Apollo 11, the Sea of Tranquility, and One Giant Leap for Mankind

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:19.920
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series journeys, Beyond the Bounds of Earth to celebrate the modest test pilot, whose ice-gold composure and courage placed humanity's first footprint on the moon.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Neil Armstrong.

5
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:36.480
[George] Born in August 1930 in rural Auglays County near Wapaconta, Ohio, Neil Alden Armstrong was mesmerized by flight from his earliest memories.

6
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.320
[George] At age two, his father took him to the Cleveland Air Races.

7
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:47.440
[George] At six, he experienced his first airplane ride aboard a Ford Trimotor Tin Goose in Warren, Ohio.

8
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:57.440
[George] An intensely focused and scholarly youth, Neil earned his student pilot's license on his 16th birthday before he had even obtained a state driver's license.

9
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:14.640
[George] Working after school at a local pharmacy, hardware store, and bakery to pay for flying lessons at $9 an hour, Neil built an operational wind tunnel in the basement of his family's home, meticulously testing scale model aircraft to study lift and drag characteristics.

10
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:29.280
[George] Enrolling at Purdue University on a Navy scholarship in 1947 to study aeronautical engineering, Armstrong's studies were interrupted in 1949 when he was called to active naval duty at Naval Air Station Pensacola.

11
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:43.840
[George] His mother Viola instilled in young Neil a quiet, reverent love for books, classical piano, and meticulous craftsmanship, while his father Stephen taught him mathematical precision during long surveying road trips across rural Ohio.

12
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:56.800
[George] By age 15, Neil had logged dozens of flying hours in an Aeronka Champion at the grass airstrip outside Wapaconta, mowing the runway lawns and cleaning grease off engine blocks to earn flying credits.

13
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:11.400
[George] His homemade wind tunnel in the basement featured an automobile fan, cardboard baffles, and wooden airfoils, demonstrating an intuitive grasp of aerodynamic flow and laminar boundary layers that stunned his high school physics teachers.

14
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:23.800
[George] Assigned to Fighter Squadron 51 aboard the aircraft carrier USS Essex during the Korean War, 20-year-old Armstrong flew 78 combat missions in the F-9 F Panther jet.

15
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:36.600
[George] During a low-altitude bombing run over Korean railway lines in September 1951, his Panther struck a hidden anti-aircraft cable strung across a valley, shearing off six feet of his right wing.

16
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:46.520
[George] Maintaining complete control over the mangled, vibrating aircraft, Armstrong nursed the jet back toward friendly lines before ejecting safely over water.

17
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:59.800
[George] Completing his degree at Purdue, Armstrong joined the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics, the predecessor to NASA, as an elite research test pilot at Edwards Air Force Base in California's Mojave Desert.

18
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] Flying over 200 distinct aircraft.

19
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:20.440
[George] Including supersonic F-100 and F-104 fighters, Armstrong piloted the experimental rocket-powered North American X-15 to an altitude of over 200,000 feet at Mach 5.7, peering down at the black curvature of the Earth from the threshold of orbital space.

20
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:35.320
[George] When his Panther jet struck the low-hanging antiaircraft cable over the Korean ravine, slicing off six feet of his starboard wing, Armstrong instinctively countered the violent aerodynamic yaw by applying full opposite aileron and rudder.

21
00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.480
[George] Nursing the crippled aircraft at 200 knots toward open water.

22
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:51.440
[George] At Edwards Air Force Base, Armstrong became known as an engineer's test pilot, preferring detailed post-flight telemetry analysis to the swaggering bravado typical of military fighter jocks.

23
00:03:51.440 --> 00:04:08.080
[George] Flying the black needle-nosed X-15 rocket plane to the edge of the mesosphere, Armstrong experienced the surreal silence of high-altitude gliding, where the sky turned ink black at midday and the blue curve of the California coastline stretched from Oregon to Baja Mexico.

24
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:20.960
[George] Selected in NASA's second astronaut group in 1962, Armstrong quickly earned a reputation among mission directors as an engineer of peerless technical competence and unshakable emotional composure.

25
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:32.560
[George] In March 1966, Armstrong commanded Gemini VIII alongside pilot David Scott, achieving the historic first orbital docking with an uncrewed IGIA target vehicle.

26
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:42.240
[George] However, minutes after docking, a stuck thruster began firing continuously, sending the coupled spacecraft into a violent, uncontrolled spin.

27
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:53.920
[George] Undocking from the Aegina only worsened the rotation, causing the Gemini capsule to spin at an alarming rate of one full revolution per second, blurring their vision and threatening fatal blackout.

28
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.320
[George] While Scott was dizzy and nauseous, Armstrong remained ice-cold.

29
00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:15.520
[George] He shut down the primary orbital maneuvering system, engaged the re-entry control thrusters, and hand flew the spinning capsule back to stable orientation, saving both astronauts' lives and demonstrating the extraordinary reflexes that made him NASA's choice to lead Apollo 11.

30
00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:26.560
[George] The near fatal malfunction during Gemini VIII demonstrated Armstrong's peerless capacity to isolate critical variables in seconds while subjected to disorienting vestibular vertigo.

31
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:41.800
[George] With the Gemini capsule tumbling through space at over 60 revolutions per minute, centrifugal force began pinning Armstrong and Scott against their harness straps, threatening to pull their blood into their extremities and induce catastrophic loss of consciousness.

32
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:56.280
[George] Recognizing that orbital maneuver thruster number 8 was jammed in the open position, Armstrong made the split-second command decision to deactivate the main attitude system and expend 75% of his re-entry fuel to halt the roll.

33
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:02.200
[George] Successfully regaining control of the spacecraft and saving the entire mission from disaster.

34
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:20.680
[George] On the morning of July 16, 1969, Neil Armstrong, command module pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module pilot Buzz Aldrin rode the elevator to the top of the monumental 363-foot Saturn V rocket at launch complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center.

35
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:35.240
[George] At 9.32 in the morning, the five massive F-1 rocket engines ignited with 7.5 million pounds of thrust, unleashing a mechanical earthquake that shook spectators miles away as the towering rocket climbed toward the heavens.

36
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:45.480
[George] Reaching translunar injection speed of 25,000 miles per hour, Apollo 11 hurtled across 240,000 miles of cislunar space.

37
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:57.880
[George] Three days later, the spacecraft entered lunar orbit, slipping behind the far side of the moon into total radio blackout, surrounded by the eerie, cratered desolation of the ancient lunar landscape.

38
00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:03.800
[George] The staggering scale of the Saturn V launch was a triumph of American industrial mobilization.

39
00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:19.040
[George] The rocket burned 15 tons of liquid oxygen and RP1 kerosene every single second, generating acoustic vibrations so powerful that birds were killed in mid-air miles away, and seismographs across the nation registered Earth tremors.

40
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.560
[George] As the massive rocket cleared the service gantry and surged into the blue Florida sky.

41
00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:36.240
[George] Armstrong's pulse rate remained at an astonishingly low 75 beats per minute, reflecting an ironclad emotional composure that NASA flight doctors had never witnessed in any other astronaut.

42
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:46.720
[George] Cruising through the translunar coast, Armstrong and Aldrin performed meticulous maintenance checks on the lunar lander, preparing for the most perilous descent in human history.

43
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:57.680
[George] On Sunday, July 20, Armstrong and Aldrin separated the lunar module Eagle from Collins Command Module Columbia and began the powered descent toward the Moon's surface.

44
00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:08.320
[George] At 30,000 feet, the guidance computer began sounding piercing program alarms, 1,202 and 1201 as computer memory overloaded.

45
00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.520
[George] Trusting flight controller Steve Bales in Houston, Armstrong kept descending.

46
00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:26.000
[George] Looking out his triangular window at 500 feet, Armstrong was horrified to see that the autopilot was guiding Eagle directly into a football field-sized crater surrounded by Volkswagen-sized boulders.

47
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] Taking semimanual control with his hand controller, Armstrong leveled the craft, tilted it forward, and flew horizontally over the boulder field, searching for a flat, clear patch of dust.

48
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.960
[George] In Houston, Capcom Charlie Duke called out, 60 seconds.

49
00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.240
[George] 30 seconds.

50
00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:52.160
[George] With fuel gauges flashing empty and dust clouds obscuring the ground, Armstrong gently set Eagle down on the lunar plane.

51
00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] Shutting off the engine, Armstrong keyed his microphone.

52
00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.240
[George] Houston, tranquility base here.

53
00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] The Eagle has landed.

54
00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.440
[George] Charlie Duke exhaled with relief.

55
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.800
[George] Roger, tranquility, we copy you on the ground.

56
00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.520
[George] You got a bunch of guys about to turn blue.

57
00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.040
[George] We're breathing again.

58
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.320
[George] Thanks a lot.

59
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.840
[George] The hair-raising descent into the sea of tranquility required extraordinary manual pilotage under extreme sensory overload.

60
00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:23.880
[George] The 1202 computer alarms repeatedly threatened to trigger an automatic abort.

61
00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.360
[George] While the lunar module's radar returned conflicting altitude readings.

62
00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:44.680
[George] Looking through his triangular cockpit window, Armstrong recognized that touching down among the jagged boulders of West Crater would tear open the fragile foil-wrapped fuel tanks or flip the lander on its side, stranding the astronauts to suffocate on the airless lunar surface.

63
00:09:44.680 --> 00:10:00.040
[George] Flying horizontally across the crater rim at 60 miles per hour, barely 50 feet off the ground, Armstrong displayed the supreme touch of a master aviator, gently kissing the lunar soil with Eagle's contact probes just seconds before fuel exhaustion.

64
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:10.760
[George] Six hours later, clad in his bulky white A7L spacesuit, Armstrong depressurized the cabin and climbed slowly down the nine-rung ladder of the lunar module.

65
00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:25.640
[George] Opening the modular equipment storage bay, he activated the black and white television camera, beaming grainy, live images across 380,000 kilometers to 650 million mesmerized human beings watching on Earth.

66
00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:32.760
[George] Pausing on the footpad, Armstrong observed that the pads had penetrated the fine powdery dust by only an inch or two.

67
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:42.040
[George] At 10:56 in the evening Eastern time, Armstrong lifted his left boot and pressed it into the virgin lunar soil, uttering the immortal sentence.

68
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.400
[George] That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

69
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:58.800
[George] Joined 20 minutes later by Buzz Aldrin, the two men explored Tranquility Base for two and a half hours, planting the American flag, collecting 48 pounds of lunar core samples and rocks.

70
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:09.040
[George] And leaving behind a stainless steel plaque inscribed with the words, here men from the planet Earth first set foot upon the moon, July 1969 AD.

71
00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.680
[George] We came in peace for all mankind.

72
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:26.000
[George] The physical sensation of moving across the lunar surface was described by Armstrong as buoyant, effortless and surreal, with the one-sixth gravity allowing the astronauts to perform sweeping, kangaroo-style leaps across the crater rims.

73
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:43.600
[George] As Armstrong examined the pristine, powdery regolith clinging to his boots like fine charcoal dust, he was struck by the stark, terrifying beauty of the lunar horizon, a razor-sharp division between the sunlit white desert and the velvety, bottomless blackness of infinite space.

74
00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:59.520
[George] Looking up into the black sky, Armstrong could see the fragile blue marble of the Earth suspended in total solitude, small enough that he could blot out his entire home planet, and every human life that had ever existed, with the simple gesture of holding up his thumb.

75
00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:15.280
[George] After successfully rejoining Collins in Columbia and splashing down safely in the Pacific Ocean on July 24, the Apollo 11 crew underwent three weeks of quarantine before embarking on a worldwide, giant leap goodwill tour across 24 nations.

76
00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:33.640
[George] While the world showered him with ticker tape parades, medals, and adulation, Armstrong remained resolutely humble, viewing himself merely as the pilot representing the 400,000 scientists, engineers, factory technicians, and seamstresses whose labor made the Apollo program possible.

77
00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:49.560
[George] Rejecting lucrative corporate board positions, political campaigns, and commercial endorsements, Armstrong resigned from NASA in 1971 to teach aerospace engineering at the University of Cincinnati and retreat to a quiet Ohio farm.

78
00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:55.560
[George] Passing away in August 2012 at age 82, his family issued a moving statement.

79
00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:12.040
[George] Honor his example of service, accomplishment, and modesty, and the next time you walk outside on a clear night and see the moon smiling down at you, think of Neil Armstrong and give him a wink.

80
00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.440
[George] Neil Armstrong's name will live as long as humanity looks to the stars.

81
00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:31.960
[George] Returning to Earth, Armstrong steadfastly refused to commercialize his immortal status, rejecting millions of dollars in corporate endorsements, television appearances, and autobiographical book contracts to maintain his personal dignity and privacy.

82
00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:45.480
[George] He chose to spend the prime of his adult life teaching undergraduate aeronautical engineering classes at the University of Cincinnati, grading problem sets by hand and treating his students with unfailing patience and warmth.

83
00:13:45.480 --> 00:14:00.040
[George] Neil Armstrong understood that his historic footprint on the Sea of Tranquility was not an individual personal triumph, but an eternal testament to the soaring potential of the human spirit when united in common purpose to explore the unknown.

84
00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.720
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

85
00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:11.320
[Rebecca] Neil Armstrong's poise, mastery of aviation, and profound humility embodied the very best of human exploration.

86
00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:21.120
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the inspiring space journey of John Glenn, from Friendship 7 to his historic return to orbit at 77.

87
00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:23.840
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

